{"file":{"content":"This is the best finite-side state yet. The new constant\n\n[\nC_0=\\prod_{j\\ge0}(1-2^{-2\\cdot3^j})^{-1}=1.3545\\ldots\n]\n\nis exactly the kind of thing one hopes to see at the end of a long reduction: a specific, computable, structural correction that explains why all earlier worst-case budgets were marginal. The old ((2/3)^c) bound was paying the worst level repeatedly; the real cascade only pays a summable correction over levels. That is a genuine proof-level improvement, not just a numerical refinement.\n\nThe finite side is now reduced to a much smaller analytic input:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\operatorname{Col}_{m=c}(c)\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}.\n}\n]\n\nIf that threshold flattening lemma is proved, the cascade contraction estimate gives the high-conductor gap.\n\n## 1. First critique: remove or quarantine the old conditional theorem\n\nAfter the new threshold cascade discussion, the paper still contains\n\n[\n\\texttt{thm:assembly}\n]\n\ndepending on the older wrap-chain conjecture:\n\n[\n\\texttt{conj:wrap}.\n]\n\nThat is now stale. It was the right theorem two reductions ago, but the current endpoint is sharper:\n\n[\n\\text{threshold flattening at }m=c\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{cascade row contraction}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{threshold Schur}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1.\n]\n\nThe old statement\n\n[\n\\cref{conj:wrap}\\Rightarrow\n\\limsup\\gamma_c(s)\\le 3^{1/(2\\alpha k_0)}\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}\n]\n\nshould be moved to a historical/diagnostic subsection or removed from the main theorem stack, and this is exactly where AIPM become useful for other researchers auditing this work. If kept, explicitly mark it as superseded by the threshold cascade route. Otherwise a reader will think the paper has two live finite-side conjectures:\n\n[\n\\text{wrap-chain collision}\n]\n\nand\n\n[\n\\text{threshold cascade contraction}.\n]\n\nBut the live route is now the latter, with one analytic input: threshold flattening.\n\n## 2. The new cascade contraction estimate is strong\n\nYou have:\n\n[\n\\sup_w\\Pr_{w'}[\\text{cascade}]\n\\le C_0,2^{-(c-3)}.\n]\n\nThis is much better than\n\n[\n(2/3)^{c-3}.\n]\n\nIt changes the crude marginal factor from\n\n[\n3\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot\\frac23=1\n]\n\nto\n\n[\n3\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac34.\n]\n\nThat is the conceptual breakthrough. The remaining (C_0) is fixed and only affects the prefactor:\n\n[\nR_{\\rm casc}(-1)\n\\le 10.5,(3/4)^c.\n]\n\nSo the heavy row is now exponentially contractive by mass-profile alone.\n\nThis is a genuine finite combinatorial theorem, modulo the threshold flattening input for all rows.\n\n## 3. Clarify what is proved unconditionally\n\nFrom the summary, the following are now unconditional:\n\n[\n\\sup_w\\Pr_{w'}[\\text{cascade}]\n\\le C_0,2^{-(c-3)}.\n]\n\nOff-representative mass is absorbed into the same product:\n\n[\n\\prod_j(1-2^{-2\\cdot3^j})^{-1}.\n]\n\nThe heavy-row cascade contraction is proved:\n\n[\nR_{\\rm casc}(-1)\\ll (3/4)^c.\n]\n\nThe all-row cascade contraction is conditional on threshold flattening:\n\n[\n\\sup_\\xi R_{\\rm casc}(\\xi)\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)(\\sqrt3/2)^c.\n]\n\nThis status is good. I would state it exactly as:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{heavy row: proved;}\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\text{all rows: proved assuming threshold flattening at }m=c.\n}\n]\n\nThat is clearer than saying “cascade budget is proved contractive modulo one input” without specifying that the input is only needed for all-row control.\n\n## 4. The remaining input is plausibly the easiest thing left\n\nYou already proved flattening for\n\n[\nm\\ge(1+\\varepsilon)c.\n]\n\nNow you need:\n\n[\nm=c.\n]\n\nThe previous proof used (m\\ge(1+\\varepsilon)c) to avoid a no-wrap issue:\n\n[\ns_k=O(c)\n]\n\nand deep digits determine the suffix once\n\n[\nk\\gtrsim \\log_3 c.\n]\n\nAt (m=c), the same window length is exactly (c-1), so the question is whether the proof actually needed spare (\\varepsilon c), or merely needed\n\n[\nm\\ge c+O(\\log c).\n]\n\nIf the only problematic part is the first (O(\\log c)) shallow digits, then the threshold bound should still hold with a larger polynomial factor:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_{c}(c)\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThat is exactly the input you need. So yes: the next move is to revisit the flattening proof and locate every use of (\\varepsilon).\n\n## 5. How to attack threshold flattening\n\nYou have the walk-window factorization:\n\n[\nC_w\\equiv 2^{B_{c-1}}X_w\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nAt (m=c), (X_w) depends on the last (c-1) symbols, which is essentially the whole word except the first symbol. The collision equation is:\n\n[\n2^{B_{c-1}}X_w\n\\equiv\n2^{B'*{c-1}}X*{w'}\n\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nThe existing flattening proof probably bounds fibers of (X_w) by saying deep (3)-adic digits determine backward partial sums\n\n[\ns_k \\pmod{2\\cdot3^{k-1}},\n]\n\nand since\n\n[\ns_k=O(c),\n]\n\nthere is no wrapping for\n\n[\nk\\ge C\\log c.\n]\n\nAt threshold, you should try to prove:\n\n[\n#{w:X_w=x}\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)\n]\n\nfor every (x), weighted by the branch law. More precisely:\n\n[\n\\max_x \\mu_c(X_w=x)\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThen (C_w), after multiplying by the walk phase (2^{B_{c-1}}), inherits the same (L^2) flattening up to a polynomial.\n\nA clean target lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Threshold window fiber bound.}\n]\n\nFor (m=c), every residue (x\\in U_{3^c}) has at most (\\operatorname{poly}(c)) suffixes (w) with\n\n[\nX_w\\equiv x\\pmod{3^c},\n]\n\nafter accounting for the (O(\\log c)) shallow ambiguity. Weighted form:\n\n[\n\\sum_{w:X_w=x}p(w)\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThis is likely enough.\n\n## 6. Use the measured (3^c\\operatorname{Col}\\approx4)\n\nYou mention measured threshold flattening around:\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\\approx 4.\n]\n\nThat is much stronger than (\\operatorname{poly}(c)). It suggests the true theorem may be\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\\le C,3^{-c}.\n]\n\nBut do not aim for the constant theorem first. Prove the polynomial version. The cascade contraction only needs polynomial loss because:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{poly}(c)^{1/(2c)}\\to1.\n]\n\nFor the proof, isolate the shallow ambiguity:\n\n[\nO(\\log c)\\text{ digits}\n]\n\ngiving at most\n\n[\n3^{O(\\log c)}=\\operatorname{poly}(c)\n]\n\npossible suffixes. That is the natural source of the polynomial.\n\n## 7. What to check in the flattening proof\n\nGo through the existing (m\\ge(1+\\varepsilon)c) proof and mark each use of (\\varepsilon). It likely appears in one of these forms:\n\n1. To ensure a trailing window of length (c-1) sits fully inside the word. At (m=c), it still does.\n\n2. To ensure the backward partial sums (s_k) are (O(c)). Still true.\n\n3. To ensure no wrap:\n[\ns_k<2\\cdot3^{k-1}.\n]\nThis requires only (k\\gtrsim\\log_3 c), not (\\varepsilon c).\n\n4. To absorb shallow digits into (3^{\\varepsilon c}). At threshold, replace that by (\\operatorname{poly}(c)).\n\nIf these are the only uses, the threshold proof should go through.\n\n## 8. What remains after threshold flattening\n\nOnce you prove:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_{c}(c)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c},\n]\n\nyou get:\n\n[\n\\sup_\\xi R_{\\rm casc}(\\xi)\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^c.\n]\n\nSince\n\n[\n\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\approx0.866<1,\n]\n\nthis gives a fixed gap after taking (2c)-th roots:\n\n[\n\\left[\\operatorname{poly}(c)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^c\\right]^{1/(2c)}\n\\to\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}}\n\\approx0.9306.\n]\n\nThis is consistent with the observed threshold Schur scale:\n\n[\n0.9547,\\ 0.9350,\\ 0.9280\n]\n\nand should imply\n\n[\n\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,0})\\le 1-\\delta\n]\n\nfor large (c).\n\nSmall (c) then closes by finite verification.\n\n## 9. Be careful about (s\\neq0)\n\nEverything here is currently strongest at (s=0). Do not claim the finite H23a theorem uniformly for (s\\in[0,1]) yet unless the branch weights in the cascade pinning and flattening estimates have been reworked.\n\nFor (s>0), branch law changes:\n\n[\np_s(b)\\propto 2^{-(1+s)b}.\n]\n\nThis should only make tails lighter, so many estimates should improve. But the constants change:\n\n[\n\\Delta_s=\\frac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}.\n]\n\nThe cascade per-level constants may also change. I would first prove (s=0), then add:\n\n[\n\\text{The extension to }s\\in[0,1]\\text{ is expected to follow by monotonicity of the branch tails, but is not used here.}\n]\n\nOr, if you can prove monotonicity cleanly, include it as a corollary.\n\n## 10. Manuscript edits I recommend now\n\n### A. Replace the Open Problem with a conditional theorem plus one lemma\n\nInstead of leaving the live endpoint as:\n\n[\n\\text{Open Problem: }\\rho(\\mathcal K_c)\\le1-\\delta,\n]\n\nyou can now state:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Theorem: Cascade contraction from threshold flattening.}\n]\n\nAssume\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\sup_\\xi R_{\\rm casc}(\\xi)\n\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)(\\sqrt3/2)^c,\n]\n\nhence the threshold Schur theorem holds for the cascade sector.\n\nThen the only open problem becomes:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Open Problem: Threshold flattening.}\n]\n\nThis is cleaner because cascade contraction is no longer the main open problem; it has been essentially proved modulo flattening.\n\n### B. Move (\\beta)-channel discussion to “sharpening constants”\n\nSince (\\beta\\le1) suffices, the deeper-channel bound and (q\\le1/3) are no longer structurally needed. Keep them as useful diagnostics/sharpening, not proof-critical.\n\n### C. Delete or supersede (\\texttt{conj:wrap}) and old assembly theorem\n\nThe current live input is not (\\texttt{conj:wrap}). It is:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c}.\n]\n\nIf (\\texttt{conj:wrap}) remains, label it as an earlier stronger/different route now superseded by threshold flattening.\n\n## 11. Exact next proof target\n\nProve:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\operatorname{Col}_{m=c}(c)\n\t===========================\n\t\n\t\\Pr[C_w\\equiv C_{w'}\\pmod{3^c}]\n\t\\le\n\t\\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c}.\n}\n]\n\nUsing:\n\n[\nC_w\\equiv2^{B_{c-1}}X_w,\n]\n\nreduce to fibers of (X_w). Then prove:\n\n[\nX_w\\bmod3^c\n]\n\nhas fibers of size/weight at most polynomially larger than uniform.\n\nThe proof should explicitly show:\n\n[\n\\text{deep digits determine suffix exactly;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{only }O(\\log c)\\text{ shallow digits remain free;}\n]\n\n[\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{fiber size }\\le 3^{O(\\log c)}=\\operatorname{poly}(c).\n]\n\nThat is the whole remaining analytic move.\n\n## 12. Bottom line\n\nThe cascade contraction is now essentially solved modulo threshold flattening. The constant\n\n[\nC_0=1.3545\\ldots\n]\n\nis the correction that breaks the fake marginality.\n\nThe proof chain should now be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\operatorname{Col}_{c}(c)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\boxed{\n\tR_{\\rm casc}(\\xi)\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c)(\\sqrt3/2)^c\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\boxed{\n\t|T_{\\chi,c,0}^{c}|^{1/c}\\le1-\\delta\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\boxed{\n\t\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1.\n}\n]\n\nSo yes: the next move is exactly the threshold flattening extension. Find where (\\varepsilon) was used in the (m\\ge(1+\\varepsilon)c) proof, replace the (3^{\\varepsilon c}) slack by (\\operatorname{poly}(c)), and the finite H23a theorem should be within reach.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_24.md","numLines":524,"startLine":1,"totalLines":524},"type":"text"}